1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,339
It took eleven months to be in a room with Martin
Sloane by accident, and Albert Wesker

2
00:00:14,359 --> 00:00:16,300
arranged every hour of it.

3
00:00:16,699 --> 00:00:22,758
Not surveillance; Sloane would have found
surveillance in a week. Wesker simply became,

4
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:28,836
over eleven months, a person who was plausibly in
the same places: a board he joined at

5
00:00:28,856 --> 00:00:35,055
a research foundation, a pair of lectures at
Georgetown, a dinner for a retiring admiral,

6
00:00:35,075 --> 00:00:41,133
a charity auction for a veterans' hospital that
Umbrella had funded since 2031 and that

7
00:00:41,153 --> 00:00:43,089
Sloane's brother-in-law ran.

8
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:49,052
By June it was not strange that they were in the
same room. By June it would have been

9
00:00:49,072 --> 00:00:51,000
strange if they had never met.

10
00:00:51,399 --> 00:00:56,129
They met at a club on Sixteenth Street, in a
corner, at nine o'clock at night.

11
00:00:56,649 --> 00:00:57,674
Eleven months.

12
00:00:58,075 --> 00:00:59,645
I beg your pardon?

13
00:01:00,045 --> 00:01:05,853
The foundation board was August. The admiral's dinner
was in November — you sat four places

14
00:01:05,873 --> 00:01:11,362
away and didn't look at me once, which was overdone.
Georgetown was February. And this

15
00:01:11,382 --> 00:01:11,875
is June.

16
00:01:12,155 --> 00:01:17,157
I've been doing this for twenty-nine years, Dr.
Wesker. When a man arranges to meet you

17
00:01:17,177 --> 00:01:22,353
by accident four times, either he wants to recruit you
or he wants to kill you, and you've

18
00:01:22,373 --> 00:01:24,605
had eleven months to do the second one.

19
00:01:25,004 --> 00:01:30,114
Wesker sat down opposite him, which he had
not been invited to do.

20
00:01:30,635 --> 00:01:34,905
You have not written anything down
about me in eight years.

21
00:01:35,635 --> 00:01:41,479
The narrator notes that this was the single most
dangerous sentence Albert Wesker ever

22
00:01:41,499 --> 00:01:47,616
spoke aloud, that he knew it, and that he spoke it in
the first ninety seconds on purpose.

23
00:01:48,135 --> 00:01:49,524
(flat) No. I haven't.

24
00:01:49,924 --> 00:01:50,774
Why not?

25
00:01:51,174 --> 00:01:56,610
Because the day I write it down it belongs to the
agency, and the agency belongs to whoever

26
00:01:56,630 --> 00:02:01,827
is running it in eighteen months, and I've watched
four things I cared about get handed

27
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:07,104
to people who didn't understand them. So I read, and
I think, and I wait to see what you

28
00:02:07,124 --> 00:02:07,344
are.

29
00:02:07,743 --> 00:02:08,973
And what am I?

30
00:02:09,373 --> 00:02:14,583
You're a capability. That's the only word that fits.
You're not a company and you're not

31
00:02:14,603 --> 00:02:20,111
a foreign power and you're certainly not a
philanthropist. You're a capability that presently

32
00:02:20,131 --> 00:02:24,508
has no owner, and my entire profession is the
acquisition of capabilities.

33
00:02:24,909 --> 00:02:31,159
Wesker regarded him for a moment with what was,
for once, unambiguous pleasure.

34
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:37,889
Mr. Sloane, in eight years you are the first
person to use the correct noun.

35
00:02:38,289 --> 00:02:44,417
The conversation lasted four hours and the narrator
will give you the spine of it, because

36
00:02:44,437 --> 00:02:50,361
it is a negotiation between two men who both knew
exactly what the other one wanted and

37
00:02:50,381 --> 00:02:53,639
neither of whom said so for the
first two hours.

38
00:02:54,159 --> 00:02:59,305
Let me tell you what I'd have brought if I'd come
here to threaten you. Nine years of

39
00:02:59,325 --> 00:03:04,471
anomalies — construction records, personnel files
with no school photographs, a Swiss

40
00:03:04,491 --> 00:03:07,509
loading bay. Nineteen things that
don't reconcile.

41
00:03:07,909 --> 00:03:15,119
Forty-one things. Agent Carrick had
forty-one, and he had them in 2033.

42
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:21,158
Carrick. Yes. That was elegant. He was the best
records man in the Bureau and you hired

43
00:03:21,178 --> 00:03:22,590
him to audit yourself.

44
00:03:22,990 --> 00:03:29,290
I hired him because he was right, and because a man
who is right is more useful inside

45
00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:35,537
an organisation than outside it, and because I have
never in my life removed a person

46
00:03:35,557 --> 00:03:36,639
for competence.

47
00:03:37,039 --> 00:03:38,891
That's an unusual policy.

48
00:03:39,290 --> 00:03:41,980
It is the only one that scales.

49
00:03:42,381 --> 00:03:44,530
Sloane turned his glass on the table.

50
00:03:45,050 --> 00:03:50,027
Here's my difficulty. I can't put you in a box
marked *foreign*, so I can't run you. I

51
00:03:50,047 --> 00:03:55,024
can't put you in a box marked *domestic*, so I can't
refer you. And every year the box

52
00:03:55,044 --> 00:04:00,369
I'd need to build gets more expensive, and every year
you do something enormous and generous

53
00:04:00,389 --> 00:04:03,680
and public that makes building it
politically impossible.

54
00:04:03,961 --> 00:04:08,616
In March of last year you saved four million
people. In April you handed the Senate a

55
00:04:08,636 --> 00:04:13,622
hostage worth two hundred and forty billion dollars.
You have made yourself un-investigable

56
00:04:13,642 --> 00:04:17,583
by being *good*, and I would like to know
whether that was the strategy.

57
00:04:17,983 --> 00:04:25,672
It was a consequence. The strategy was to build
something that worked. But yes, I understood

58
00:04:25,692 --> 00:04:32,877
the consequence in 2027, and I have optimised for it
ever since, and I am not going to

59
00:04:32,897 --> 00:04:35,894
pretend otherwise to a professional.

60
00:04:36,569 --> 00:04:38,614
All right. Then what do you want from me?

61
00:04:39,012 --> 00:04:43,856
And here Albert Wesker did the thing he had
planned for eleven months.

62
00:04:44,377 --> 00:04:45,127
Nothing.

63
00:04:45,528 --> 00:04:46,447
(flat) Don't.

64
00:04:46,846 --> 00:04:53,604
I am entirely serious. I do not want access. I do
not want product. I do not want you

65
00:04:53,624 --> 00:05:00,622
to tell me anything your service knows, and if you
offered it to me I would decline, and

66
00:05:00,642 --> 00:05:02,776
I would decline in writing.

67
00:05:03,175 --> 00:05:04,627
Then why am I here?

68
00:05:05,026 --> 00:05:11,336
Because in about six years an agency of this
government is going to decide that Umbrella

69
00:05:11,356 --> 00:05:17,809
is a target, and it is going to be wrong, and the
decision is going to be made by somebody

70
00:05:17,829 --> 00:05:23,995
who has read a summary. I want there to be one
person in that building who has spent a

71
00:05:24,015 --> 00:05:30,181
decade reading the primary material and who can say,
with authority, *I have looked at

72
00:05:30,201 --> 00:05:34,137
this for ten years and it is not
what you think it is.*

73
00:05:34,416 --> 00:05:41,777
I do not want an asset, Mr. Sloane. I want an
*informed adult* in a room I will never

74
00:05:41,797 --> 00:05:43,166
be allowed into.

75
00:05:43,567 --> 00:05:45,537
Martin Sloane sat very still.

76
00:05:46,057 --> 00:05:51,179
That's not a recruitment. That's worse. If I say yes
to that, I haven't been bought, I've

77
00:05:51,199 --> 00:05:54,358
been — persuaded. And I'll never be
able to tell which.

78
00:05:54,757 --> 00:05:56,668
No. You will not.

79
00:05:57,507 --> 00:05:58,725
And what do I get?

80
00:05:59,125 --> 00:06:05,844
You get the truth. All of it, tonight, once, with no
conditions, and you may do anything

81
00:06:05,864 --> 00:06:11,895
you like with it. That is the entire offer and
it does not improve if you wait.

82
00:06:12,295 --> 00:06:13,315
He told him.

83
00:06:13,714 --> 00:06:19,066
Not as much as he had told Carrick — there was no
diner recording, no admission about

84
00:06:19,086 --> 00:06:24,879
Newark — but the shape of it: that Umbrella's growth
was not financed in any way a financial

85
00:06:24,899 --> 00:06:30,376
analyst would recognise, that the buildings did not
have construction histories because

86
00:06:30,396 --> 00:06:35,811
they had not been constructed, that there were three
facilities on earth that appeared

87
00:06:35,831 --> 00:06:41,182
on no plan, and that the thing which made it all
possible was not a technology, not a

88
00:06:41,202 --> 00:06:42,824
network, and not a person.

89
00:06:43,345 --> 00:06:45,992
(very slow) You're telling me
there's an intelligence.

90
00:06:46,391 --> 00:06:48,961
I am telling you there is a
*counterparty*.

91
00:06:49,362 --> 00:06:50,082
Whose?

92
00:06:50,482 --> 00:06:57,298
I have asked that question four hundred times in
eight years. I have received an answer

93
00:06:57,318 --> 00:07:03,192
twice, and both answers were the same three
words: *the panel is accurate.*

94
00:07:03,591 --> 00:07:06,221
Sloane looked into the fire for
a long moment.

95
00:07:06,742 --> 00:07:12,132
You know what the terrifying part is? Not the
building. Not the memories. It's that you've

96
00:07:12,152 --> 00:07:17,363
told me, and there is nothing whatsoever I can do
with it. If I file this I'm finished.

97
00:07:17,383 --> 00:07:21,572
If I bury it I'm yours. You've made the
truth into a — a kind of trap.

98
00:07:21,971 --> 00:07:29,391
Yes. I have done that four times now and it works
every time, and I will tell you exactly

99
00:07:29,411 --> 00:07:36,997
why: because the truth about me is too large for any
institution to carry, and institutions

100
00:07:37,017 --> 00:07:39,421
are the only things you have.

101
00:07:40,451 --> 00:07:48,231
Roster. New. Martin J. Sloane. Human. Loyalty, forty
per cent. He has not agreed to anything

102
00:07:48,251 --> 00:07:55,607
and will not. He will never again permit a
subordinate to write the word hostile beside

103
00:07:55,627 --> 00:07:59,083
your name. Eight hundred thousand Points.

104
00:07:59,673 --> 00:08:05,733
Six days later Wesker spent nine hundred thousand
Points in the Intelligence tab, and

105
00:08:05,753 --> 00:08:11,743
the narrator will describe what he bought because
it explains the next twenty years.

106
00:08:12,892 --> 00:08:21,201
Purchase. Asset Network, United States. Nine hundred
thousand. Forty-one persons in government,

107
00:08:21,221 --> 00:08:28,652
media and finance. Each believes they were
recruited by someone else, for an ordinary

108
00:08:28,672 --> 00:08:36,192
reason, at a plausible moment in their own past.
None has ever heard the word Umbrella

109
00:08:36,212 --> 00:08:37,594
in that context.

110
00:08:38,184 --> 00:08:40,694
None of them knows they work for me.

111
00:08:41,095 --> 00:08:47,482
None of them works for you. Each of them works for
a version of their own career that

112
00:08:47,502 --> 00:08:53,965
has always included a particular set of instincts.
They will not do what you ask. They

113
00:08:53,985 --> 00:09:01,052
will do what they have always believed, and what they
have always believed will be convenient.

114
00:09:01,836 --> 00:09:06,625
(very cold) That is the most frightening
item you have ever sold me.

115
00:09:07,025 --> 00:09:11,303
It is the fourth most frightening
item in that tab.

116
00:09:11,703 --> 00:09:16,860
He never bought the other three. He looked at them,
once a year, for thirty years, and

117
00:09:16,880 --> 00:09:21,193
did not buy them, and the narrator has chosen
not to say what they were.

118
00:09:21,594 --> 00:09:23,724
The voice inside him, unasked.

119
00:09:24,244 --> 00:09:25,925
You are squeamish.

120
00:09:26,323 --> 00:09:33,148
I am *disciplined*. There is a difference, and it is
the reason I am here in 2034 with

121
00:09:33,168 --> 00:09:39,913
a trillion dollars and a Senate that likes me, and
you were in a bunker at fifty-six.

122
00:09:40,589 --> 00:09:45,395
That is the first time you have ever
used it as a weapon.

123
00:09:45,794 --> 00:09:48,524
It is the first time you have deserved it.

124
00:09:48,924 --> 00:09:54,553
The narrator notes that the passenger did not speak
again for nine days, which is the

125
00:09:54,573 --> 00:10:00,268
longest silence between them in sixty-five years,
and that when he did speak it was to

126
00:10:00,288 --> 00:10:06,049
point out — correctly — that Wesker had scheduled a
public appearance at the wrong time

127
00:10:06,069 --> 00:10:06,514
of day.

128
00:10:06,914 --> 00:10:12,772
Owen Carrick had been Director of Internal Security
for seven months, and he came up to

129
00:10:12,792 --> 00:10:16,555
the sixth floor with a folder on the
last night of June.

130
00:10:17,075 --> 00:10:18,765
(flat) Asset Network One.

131
00:10:19,164 --> 00:10:22,335
That is not in any system you
have access to.

132
00:10:22,735 --> 00:10:28,697
(flat) No. But four hundred and eleven Points came off
an internal ledger that Pell reconciles

133
00:10:28,717 --> 00:10:34,361
monthly, and nine hundred thousand of something came
off it in June, and I've spent three

134
00:10:34,381 --> 00:10:39,899
weeks working out what the something is. I can't see
the panels. I can see the *holes*.

135
00:10:40,298 --> 00:10:46,633
Wesker put down his pen and, the narrator notes,
looked genuinely pleased for the second

136
00:10:46,653 --> 00:10:47,788
time in a month.

137
00:10:48,308 --> 00:10:51,158
Go on, then. Tell me no.

138
00:10:51,558 --> 00:10:57,138
Forty-one people in government and media who think
they woke up one day with a set of

139
00:10:57,158 --> 00:11:02,738
opinions. That's not intelligence work. That's not
even corruption. Corruption leaves

140
00:11:02,758 --> 00:11:07,876
a man who knows he was bought — and a man who
knows he was bought can confess.

141
00:11:08,157 --> 00:11:10,587
You've built something nobody
can confess to.

142
00:11:11,427 --> 00:11:12,297
Yes.

143
00:11:12,697 --> 00:11:17,986
Then I'm telling you no. Formally. It's in the
folder, it's dated, and I'd like it in

144
00:11:18,006 --> 00:11:20,547
the record you keep of the things I said.

145
00:11:20,947 --> 00:11:27,038
Wesker took the folder and read it — all twenty-two
pages, in front of him, while Carrick

146
00:11:27,058 --> 00:11:32,876
stood there — and then put it in the drawer of the
desk where four such folders would

147
00:11:32,896 --> 00:11:33,975
eventually live.

148
00:11:34,495 --> 00:11:36,846
Noted, and overruled.

149
00:11:37,245 --> 00:11:38,456
(flat) Understood.

150
00:11:38,855 --> 00:11:45,586
Mr. Carrick. Do you want to know why I am not
going to take the network apart?

151
00:11:45,985 --> 00:11:48,383
I'd rather you told me than let me guess.

152
00:11:48,783 --> 00:11:55,593
Because in 2040 somebody in that government is going to
decide to move against this company,

153
00:11:55,613 --> 00:12:02,126
and the difference between forty-one people with
convenient instincts and no such people

154
00:12:02,146 --> 00:12:08,436
is the difference between a bad six months and a
war. I am not buying influence. I am

155
00:12:08,456 --> 00:12:09,773
buying *friction*.

156
00:12:10,173 --> 00:12:12,383
Carrick thought about that for a moment.

157
00:12:12,903 --> 00:12:16,773
That's the first answer you've given me
that I can't take apart.

158
00:12:17,173 --> 00:12:24,415
It is also true. Those two things will not always
coincide, and I would ask you to keep

159
00:12:24,435 --> 00:12:27,002
watching for the day they stop.
